window.SKIM_PAPERS["1512.00567"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "label-smoothing-regularization", "name": "Label-Smoothing Regularization (LSR)", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Replace the one-hot training target with a mixture of that one-hot target and a fixed label distribution, so the correct class gets mass $1-\\epsilon$ and the rest of the mass $\\epsilon$ is spread over all classes. The paper calls this label-smoothing regularization, or LSR.", "explanation": "Start with the ordinary setup. For a training example $x$ the model produces a distribution $p(k)$ over labels $k \\in \\{1 \\dots K\\}$, and training normally minimizes the cross entropy $\\ell = -\\sum_{k=1}^{K} q(k)\\log p(k)$ against the ground-truth distribution $q$. With a single correct label $y$, that target is the Dirac delta $q(k) = \\delta_{k,y}$: one at $k=y$, zero elsewhere.\n\nLSR changes only the target. It fixes a smoothing parameter $\\epsilon$ and a label distribution $u(k)$ that does not depend on $x$, and replaces $q$ with $$q'(k \\mid x) = (1-\\epsilon)\\,\\delta_{k,y} + \\epsilon\\, u(k).$$ The paper suggests using the prior distribution over labels for $u$, and in its experiments uses the uniform distribution $u(k) = 1/K$, giving $$q'(k) = (1-\\epsilon)\\,\\delta_{k,y} + \\frac{\\epsilon}{K}.$$\n\nRead the two cases off that formula. The correct class receives $1-\\epsilon+\\epsilon/K$, and every other class receives $\\epsilon/K$. The smoothed mass is spread over all $K$ classes including the correct one, not only over the wrong ones.\n\nThe paper also gives a sampling reading of the same formula: set the label to $y$, then with probability $\\epsilon$ replace it with a draw from $u$. The smoothed target is the marginalized effect of dropping the label that often during training.\n\nNothing else about training changes. The loss is still cross entropy, now $-\\sum_k q'(k)\\log p(k)$, and the per-logit gradient keeps the form $\\partial \\ell / \\partial z_k = p(k) - q'(k)$.\n\nWhat changes is what the target can ask for. Because every $q'(k)$ has a positive lower bound, the target can no longer be matched by driving one logit far above the others.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 7, \"Model Regularization via Label Smoothing\" (pages 6-7); the two displayed equations for $q'(k\\mid x)$.", "ownerPaper": "1512.00567", "sources": {"paperId": "1512.00567", "note": "Section 7, \"Model Regularization via Label Smoothing\" (pages 6-7); the two displayed equations for $q'(k\\mid x)$.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "lsr-as-two-cross-entropy-losses", "name": "LSR as a pair of cross-entropy losses", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Because cross entropy is linear in the target, smoothing splits the loss into the ordinary one-hot cross entropy plus a term that pulls the predicted distribution toward the prior $u$. The second term carries relative weight $\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)$.", "explanation": "Start from the smoothed target $q' = (1-\\epsilon)q + \\epsilon u$ and substitute it into the cross entropy. Cross entropy is linear in the target distribution, so the substitution splits cleanly:\n\n$$H(q',p) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{K} q'(k)\\log p(k) = (1-\\epsilon)H(q,p) + \\epsilon H(u,p).$$\n\nRead the right-hand side as a pair of losses rather than one. The first term is the usual cross entropy on the true label, the loss that was already there. The second, $H(u,p)$, penalizes deviation of the predicted distribution $p$ from the fixed prior $u$.\n\nTheir relative weight is $\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)$: divide the second coefficient by the first and that is what remains.\n\nThat second term can equivalently be read as a KL divergence. Since $H(u,p) = D_{KL}(u\\|p) + H(u)$, and $H(u)$ is a constant that does not affect gradients, penalizing cross entropy against $u$ and penalizing divergence from $u$ give the same training signal.\n\nWhen $u$ is uniform, $H(u,p)$ measures how far $p$ is from uniform. The paper notes that negative entropy $-H(p)$ would measure something similar but not equivalent, and says it did not try that.\n\nThe decomposition is also the cheapest way to implement [[label-smoothing-regularization|LSR]]. Keep the existing cross-entropy code and add the prior term, or simply build the smoothed target vector $q'$ and feed it to the same loss.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 7, the displayed identity $H(q',p) = (1-\\epsilon)H(q,p) + \\epsilon H(u,p)$ and the paragraph following it.", "ownerPaper": "1512.00567", "sources": {"paperId": "1512.00567", "note": "Section 7, the displayed identity $H(q',p) = (1-\\epsilon)H(q,p) + \\epsilon H(u,p)$ and the paragraph following it.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "overconfidence-and-bounded-logit-gradient", "name": "Why one-hot targets encourage overconfidence", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "A one-hot target is only approached as the correct logit grows far larger than the rest, which the paper argues causes overfitting and, given the bounded logit gradient, reduces the model's ability to adapt.", "explanation": "Set the target to a one-hot vector, $q(k) = \\delta_{k,y}$. Then the cross-entropy minimum is not attainable at finite logits. It is only approached in the limit, as $z_y \\gg z_k$ for all $k \\neq y$.\n\nThe paper flags two consequences of that.\n\nThe first is about generalization. If the model learns to put full probability on the ground-truth label of every training example, there is no guarantee that behavior generalizes.\n\nThe second is about adaptability. Training keeps pushing the gap between the largest logit and the others wider. The gradient with respect to the logits is $\\partial \\ell / \\partial z_k = p(k) - q(k)$, which is bounded between $-1$ and $1$. Given that bound, the paper argues the widening gap reduces the model's ability to adapt. It describes the mechanism intuitively as the model becoming too confident about its predictions.\n\n[[label-smoothing-regularization|LSR]] targets exactly this. Every $q'(k)$ has a positive lower bound, so a prediction that sends one probability toward 1 and the rest toward 0 incurs a large cross entropy against $q'$. The largest logit is therefore prevented from running far ahead of the others.\n\nThe paper is explicit that this is a trade. It says the method is not what you want if the goal is to maximize the log-likelihood of the training labels, but that it regularizes the model and makes it more adaptable.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 7, the paragraphs preceding the definition of $q'$ (the Dirac-delta case and the two problems it causes), plus the note after the $q'$ equation on positive lower bounds.", "ownerPaper": "1512.00567", "sources": {"paperId": "1512.00567", "note": "Section 7, the paragraphs preceding the definition of $q'$ (the Dirac-delta case and the two problems it causes), plus the note after the $q'$ equation on positive lower bounds.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "smoothing-parameter-epsilon", "name": "Choosing $\\epsilon$ and the prior $u$", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "LSR has two knobs: the smoothing parameter $\\epsilon$ and the fixed distribution $u$. The paper's reported setting is $\\epsilon = 0.1$ with uniform $u(k)=1/K$ over $K = 1000$ ImageNet classes.", "explanation": "[[label-smoothing-regularization|LSR]] has two knobs, and they do different jobs. $\\epsilon$ controls how much target mass leaves the correct label. $u$ decides where that mass goes.\n\nThe paper proposes using the prior distribution over labels as $u$, and in practice uses the uniform distribution. With $u$ pinned that way and $K$ fixed by the dataset, the whole scheme reduces to a single hyperparameter, $\\epsilon$.\n\nIts ImageNet setting is $K = 1000$, $u(k) = 1/1000$, and $\\epsilon = 0.1$. Work the resulting target out: the true class keeps $1 - \\epsilon = 0.9$ plus its share of the redistributed mass, $\\epsilon/K = 0.1/1000 = 0.0001$, so $0.9 + 0.0001$; every other class receives that same $0.0001$.\n\nReported effect: a consistent improvement of about 0.2% absolute on both top-1 and top-5 error for ILSVRC 2012, listed as one of the cumulative ingredients in the paper's ablation table.\n\nOne thing to keep in mind when reading loss curves. With a smoothed target the training loss has a floor: the minimum of $-\\sum_k q'(k)\\log p(k)$ is the entropy $H(q')$, reached at $p = q'$. So the loss value is no longer comparable to an unsmoothed run, and the model is calibrated toward $q'$ rather than toward the true labels.\n\nA model trained this way is deliberately less confident, which can cost likelihood-style metrics even where it helps accuracy. The paper frames the method as trading training-label log-likelihood for regularization.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 7, final paragraph ($K=1000$, $u(k)=1/1000$, $\\epsilon=0.1$, ~0.2% absolute gain); Table 3 (\"Label Smoothing\" row in the cumulative ablation).", "ownerPaper": "1512.00567", "sources": {"paperId": "1512.00567", "note": "Section 7, final paragraph ($K=1000$, $u(k)=1/1000$, $\\epsilon=0.1$, ~0.2% absolute gain); Table 3 (\"Label Smoothing\" row in the cumulative ablation).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "refs": {"accessed": []}};
